# Deep Learning-Based Image Classification for Automated Detection of Plant Diseases

This project explores the use of **deep learning models** for automated detection of plant diseases in smart agriculture.

## 🌱 Overview
Traditional plant disease detection methods are manual and error-prone. This project develops an AI-based automated system using image classification on the **PlantVillage dataset**, focusing on three major crops: **tomato, potato, and bell pepper**.

## ⚙️ Methodology
Two models were implemented and compared:
1. **Custom CNN (Baseline)** – Achieved 85.75% validation accuracy.  
2. **ResNet50 Transfer Learning Model** – Fine-tuned on PlantVillage data; achieved 99.25% validation and 99.18% test accuracy.

Both models were trained using **PyTorch** in **Google Colab** with GPU acceleration.

## 📊 Key Results
| Model | Validation Accuracy | Test Accuracy |
|--------|----------------------|----------------|
| CNN | 85.75% | 84.92% |
| ResNet50 | 99.25% | 99.18% |

The ResNet50 model demonstrated strong generalization on unseen data but faced minor issues with real-world field images, emphasizing the need for more diverse datasets.

## 💡 Conclusions
Deep learning—particularly transfer learning—proves highly effective for plant disease classification.  
Future work should focus on **lightweight, field-deployable models** and **domain adaptation** for real-world agricultural applications.

